ue": {"coverimg": "engenn6le14ob05re2img05.jpg", "audiosrc": "engenn6le14ob05re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "engenn6le14ob05re2img05.jpg", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"time": "100", "image": "engenn6le14ob05re2img05.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Good job, everyone - that was fun! I thought I would begin with a simple exercise. Now we will consider cultural differences that can be more complicated.</p>Firstly, North Americans are expected to present their ideas in a formal and rational manner. At a business meeting, emotional speakers may be considered dishonest or unreliable. In contrast, South Americans may express their feelings about a fact more freely through the voice, word choice or body language."}, {"time": "30000", "image": "engenn6le14ob05re2img06.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Regarding punctuality, North Americans consider it inappropriate to be late for any meeting. They usually arrive shortly before the agreed time. It is appropriate to refuse to chat with a person one meets on the way because it is important not to keep anyone waiting. However, being dismissive may be offensive to Latinos."}, {"time": "52000", "image": "engenn6le14ob05re2img07.jpg", "id": 3, "text": "My last point concerns ideas of honesty. North Americans value direct speech. For instance, it is acceptable for colleagues or employers to correct or disagree with one another, whereas a Latino may find this way of talking offensive. Unlike the North American, whose words must give a direct meaning, a Latino may interpret words for indirect meanings. North Americans may strengthen their meaning with expression, but, unlike the Latino, not contradict it.<br/>In summary, it is important to understand cultural differences and expectations to prevent misunderstanding. Thank you for your attention. Now, the floor is open for questions."}]}}], "template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": []}]}]}